  "responseTimeMs": 19133,
  "answer": "### Short Answer\nThe VAT rate on the importation of goods used to manufacture export products is 12%. This is based on the total value determined by the Bureau of Customs, which includes customs duties and other charges.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\nThe applicable authority is Section 107 of the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC), which states that a value-added tax equivalent to 12% shall be levied on every importation of goods. This is further supported by Revenue Regulations No. 16-2005.\n\n### Interpretation\n\n### Practical Meaning\nFor businesses importing goods to be used in the production of export products, they should prepare to pay a VAT of 12% upon importation. This VAT is a cost that must be factored into the overall pricing and financial planning of the business. However, if the goods are ultimately used for export, the business may be eligible for a refund or credit of the VAT paid, as export sales are typically zero-rated under the VAT system.",
